Emma (Support): Sirocco Pay reporting intermittent 429s since ~03:10 UTC. Customer says it comes in short waves every minute. Can we triage?

sirocco_ops: Hey — ~5-10% of requests get 429s on the minute boundary. We're on Dedicated. No deploy changes. Attached headers from a failing request: `X-RateLimit-Limit: 600`, `X-RateLimit-Remaining: 0`, `Retry-After: 30`.

Liam (SRE): @sirocco_ops thanks — can you paste a tiny timestamped sample of request rates? Also confirm whether keys are per-team or per-subaccount. If Remaining hits 0 at minute boundary that's a classic RPM window refill issue.

sirocco_ops: pasted a short log snippet: `03:09:58 worker-12 POST /v1/generate 200`, `03:10:00 worker-01 POST /v1/generate 429`, `03:10:01 worker-37 POST /v1/generate 429`, `03:10:10 requests drop back to 200`. Pattern repeats each minute.

Natalie (Eng): That looks like many clients flushing at the same minute boundary — average throughput might be within quota but instantaneous burst exceeds RPM. Also check TPM if they're streaming — long-lived streams count toward token throughput differently.

Emma (Support): Customer sent repro steps: 60 workers, each normally ~1 req/sec. Every minute they do a batch flush that spikes to ~120 concurrent requests for ~8-12s. No jitter/backoff in the client.

Natalie (Eng): Ok — two paths: 1) Client-side smoothing (stagger flushes, add jitter, reduce concurrency) which fixes root cause. 2) Short-term server-side mitigation: quota bump or enable reserved burst capacity on Dedicated. Recommend both.

Liam (SRE): Quick mitigation guidance to give customer: implement Retry-After + exponential backoff + jitter. Example JS snippet to include: 

```js
async function fetchWithRetry(url, opts){
  for(let i=0;i<5;i++){
    const res = await fetch(url, opts);
    if(res.status !== 429) return res;
    const ra = res.headers.get('Retry-After');
    const wait = ra ? parseInt(ra)*1000 : Math.pow(2, i) * 1000;
    await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, wait + Math.random()*200));
  }
  throw new Error('max retries');
}
```

Natalie (Eng): Also advise them to limit per-worker concurrency (eg max 4 concurrent requests) and add a small random offset to their flush window (+/- 30s). That typically smooths minute-aligned spikes.

sirocco_ops: We do have some streaming sessions open. Could streaming cause this even with low request rate?

Natalie (Eng): Yes — streaming can increase token-per-minute (TPM) load because each response chunk consumes tokens and you may have many concurrent streams. Check both RPM and TPM headers in your logs. If TPM is the culprit we should look at token consumption per session.

Emma (Support): I'll open an internal escalation for a temporary quota bump and schedule a quick screen-share to walk through the smoothing changes.

ratebot: deploy-bot: Created ticket INC-98765 for quota-bump-request.

Liam (SRE): Action items for support reply: 1) Share the JS retry snippet and sample curl `-I` to capture headers, 2) Ask customer to add +/-30s jitter to their flush and cap concurrency per worker, 3) Request a 1-hour live log tail (if they agree) so we can confirm fix, 4) I will lobby for a temporary burst increase while they patch.

Emma (Support): I'll send that to Sirocco now and attach a short KB blurb about "minute-boundary burst behavior". Will include example curl: `curl -i -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" https://api.redwood.example/v1/generate` to capture `X-RateLimit-*` headers.

Natalie (Eng): Note for internal follow-up: consider adding a small product-side client guidance doc and a console alert when a key gets 429 spikes concentrated at window boundaries.

sirocco_ops: Thanks — implementing jitter and concurrency limit now, will report back in ~45m. :thumbsup:

Emma (Support): Great — marking this thread as in-progress. Will update INC-98765 with logs and next steps. :white_check_mark:
